<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Volga</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Volga River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Volga is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, flowing through central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. It was a major artery of trade linking the Baltic and northern forests with the Caspian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the Islamic world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a> controlled its lower course, with its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atil">Atil</a> near the river's mouth, and profited greatly from the commerce passing along it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/volga.jpg" alt="Volga" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexxx1979 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yaroslavl._Volga_River._Cathedral_of_the_Dormition_P5212700_2200.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early seventeenth century the Oirats were divided and under pressure from the Khalkha Mongols and the Kazakhs; some groups migrated west to the <b>Volga</b>, where they became the Kalmyks, and others moved to the Kokonor region and Tibet as the Khoshut.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1235 an assembly (quriltai) at Karakorum ordered a great campaign to the west, and from 1236 Batu, with the general Subutai and princes of all branches of the ruling house, conquered <b>Volga</b> Bulgaria, the Kipchak (Cuman) steppe and the principalities of Rus'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Furs, wax, honey and leather were the main exports, traded through Novgorod to the Hanseatic League, down the <b>Volga</b> to Kazan and the steppe, and south towards the Crimea, where Moscow's merchants known as surozhane dealt in silks and other luxuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Huns were a nomadic people first reported east of the <b>Volga River</b>, in what was then Scythia; their origins are uncertain, probably in Central Asia, and classical sources say they appeared in Europe suddenly around 370.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It was established by Turkic-speaking nomadic elites who consolidated power across territories stretching from the lower <b>Volga</b> to the northern Caucasus and the steppes north of the Black Sea, subjugating or displacing rivals such as the Bulgars of Kubrat's Old Great Bulgaria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Scandinavian traders and warriors, known as Rus' or Varangians, moved down the Volkhov, Dnieper and <b>Volga</b> to exchange furs and slaves for Byzantine goods and Islamic silver, and a Rus' polity, sometimes called the Rus' Khaganate, existed in the north by the 830s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
